        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Yangshao culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The middle reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and the neighbouring provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>. Key sites: Yangshao (Mianchi County, Henan), Banpo near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi’an</a>, Jiangzhai, Beishouling, Dadiwan, Xishan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a> and Xipo.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 5000–3000 BCE: Early Yangshao or Banpo phase c. 5000–4000 BCE, Middle Yangshao or Miaodigou phase c. 4000–3500 BCE, Late Yangshao c. 3500–3000 BCE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> farming; pig keeping; painted pottery with human faces, animals and geometric designs; jiandiping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a>; ditch-enclosed villages; semi-subterranean houses; millet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> beer; the first rammed-earth wall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; the oldest known depiction of a dragon</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated settlements and burials since 1921; pottery styles and stratigraphy (the Hougang site); residues and tools; studies of diet. No writing: marks on pottery at Banpo and Jiangzhai have been read by a few as numerals or early signs, a reading not widely accepted.</span></div>
        </div>
