    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Yangshao people farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, along the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and kept pigs and dogs; over two thousand years their region became a fully settled farming society.</li><li>Their potters, working without a wheel, made fine painted wares whose fish, face and geometric designs changed over time and spread west to the Majiayao culture and beyond.</li><li>Villages of houses round a central square were joined in the later phases by settlement hierarchies, large public buildings and, at Xishan, the first rammed-earth wall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A dragon figure from the Yangshao culture, dating to the fifth millennium BCE, is the world’s oldest known depiction of a dragon.</p></aside>
    </section>

